      ],
      "overview": "Session d02 discussed only one paper, cited first and last: 2004.00809 \"Mapping Languages and Demographics with Georeferenced Corpora.\" Recurring topics: the finding that Twitter data gives better predictions of each country's language inventory (fewer missing languages), more false positives in Twitter data, better representation of North America and Oceania, the GDP- vs internet-weighted population matching and the wealthier-urban-user interpretations, geolocation/GDP confound, 1万城市50km spatial collection design, r=0.60 vs r=0.49 correlations, and the 5% language threshold with sparse ground truth. Unresolved references: whether the authors state this as an explicit hypothesis (not found in excerpts), causal reasons for the better prediction, whether Twitter users in overrepresented African countries are locals or visitors, sources of false positives (immigration, tourism, business), and a rural-centered collection open question. No other papers referenced.",
      "paper_order": [
        "2004.00809"
      ],
